# Language pipelines

A served language model inherits `LanguagePipeline` in `src/favlo/language_pipeline.py`. That class extends `Pipeline`. A media pipeline does not inherit it. A failed pipeline does not. The class is the disconnect shell around one response. It does not encode a prompt, sample a token, or parse a tool call.

One concrete class per package: `class Pipeline(LanguagePipeline)`. Implement abstract `handle_responses`. That method reads a Responses body and writes a Response, including its sampler and its stream. Bind `handle_responses = Pipeline()`. The daemon calls that instance, which runs the disconnect shell and then the subclass method. Do not keep a `_Hooks` middle class or a package `handle_chat`. `RequestClosed` subclasses `ClientClosed`.

## Chat Completions

Pipelines implement the Responses API only. Do not bind `handle_chat`. Do not translate Chat Completions bodies, write `chat.completion` / `chat.completion.chunk` objects, or wrap the reply in `ChatWire` inside a pipeline. The listener owns that wrapper once: `POST /v1/chat/completions` runs `chat_to_responses`, dispatches the module's `handle_responses` instance, and `ChatWire` (or `ResponseWire` on `POST /v1/responses`) lives in `favlo.responses`.

`POST /v1/responses` is the generation route. The pipeline's `handle_responses` method reads a Responses body and writes a Response. Another pipeline's chat, stream, or markup parser is not a reference. Read the OpenAI Responses fields and this model's own card.

The fields below are the listener Chat Completions translation. `content` is the reply. It is null when the model only calls a tool. `tool_calls` are the calls, in the OpenAI function shape. `reasoning` is the trace when the model wrote one. `reasoning_content` is that same text. An empty thought is not that field. `finish_reason` is `stop`, `length`, or `tool_calls`. The reply fills `usage` with the OpenAI token fields and `max_prompt_tokens` before the body is encoded. A pipeline may set `cached_tokens`, `context_length`, and `max_prompt_tokens` on the Response (including `_chat_usage` for the wire). Missing standard fields are filled.

`chat_to_responses` maps the chat body onto a Responses request (`reasoning_content` becomes a reasoning item, effort and `preserve_thinking` pass through), the daemon runs the same Responses instance, and `ChatWire` writes the Response back as a chat completion. Markers stay out of `content`. A call becomes `tool_calls`. An open trace stays `reasoning_content` and does not become the reply. A call that starts before the trace closes ends the trace. The marker strings belong to that model. They are not taken from a sibling pipeline. Do not drop `reasoning_content` on the way in: a missing reasoning item breaks the stored prefix when `preserve_thinking` is on.

Wrong: `handle_chat` on a language pipeline, a Chat Completions encoder in the package, or a stream that emits `chat.completion.chunk` from the pipeline.

Right: the pipeline writes Responses events and Response JSON; the listener wraps Chat Completions over that path.

`on_open` is the work that already sits outside the try. qwen36 and qwen38-27b reset `_halted` and set `runtime._stop` there. Flash Next only calls `open_request` there. `prepare` is inside the outer try and outside the disconnect try. Only Flash Next uses it, for the tool fields and the sampler. `on_finish` keeps that pipeline's current order and always runs after `prepare`. Hooks are called directly. Do not swallow a hook exception.

A new abstract method on `LanguagePipeline` updates every served language subclass in that same change. A new abstract method on `Pipeline` updates every subclass of every seam.

## Variants

A variant is a named card under one registry row. Registration copies `VARIANTS` and `DEFAULT_VARIANT` from that pipeline's `sampling.py`. The public model id is the row's alias when it stores one, otherwise the folder. `GET /v1/models` and `resolve` use that public id only. Listed ids are `<id>`, `<id>:thinking`, `<id>:instruct`, and any other registered suffix. The packed folder name is not a request id when the row has an alias. A pipeline stem is not a resolve target unless it is that public id.

Each variant card must set `temperature`, `top_p`, `top_k`, and `presence_penalty`. It may also set `reasoning_effort`. Those are the only fields a variant stores today. Choosing `<id>:suffix` loads that card's sampling defaults onto `Selection.sampling` and, when present, that card's `reasoning_effort` onto `Selection.reasoning_effort`. A bare `<id>` uses `DEFAULT_VARIANT` for sampling defaults and for the omitted-request effort when that default card stores `reasoning_effort`. The request body still overrides sampling fields one by one. Changing effort does not swap the sampling card. Do not infer thinking from the suffix spelling (`:instruct`, `:thinking`). Read the stored `reasoning_effort` (or the request).

Wrong: list both the folder and the alias, resolve the folder when an alias exists, treat `:instruct` as thinking off by name, or put non-card fields only on the parent while claiming the suffix changes them.

Right: one public id per row, variant cards under that id, and omitted effort taken from the selected card's `reasoning_effort` when it stores one.

## Reasoning effort

`reasoning.effort` on the Responses body (and Chat Completions `reasoning_effort` / `enable_thinking` mapped onto it) wins when the request sets it. Read the official model card for which effort names that pipeline actually supports. Accept every listed OpenAI effort (`none`, `minimal`, `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max`). Map an unsupported name to the next higher supported value. When no higher supported value exists, fall back to the next lower supported value. Example: if the card supports only `none` and `high`, then `low` and `medium` become `high`, and `xhigh` and `max` fall back to `high`. Gemma 4 is on or off only: the Gemini API names those levels `minimal` (off) and `high` (on), so `SUPPORTED_EFFORTS` is that pair; `none` steps up to `minimal`, and `low` / `medium` / `xhigh` / `max` resolve to `high`.

When the request omits effort, resolve in this order: the selected variant's `reasoning_effort` (the suffix card, or the default variant on a bare id), then an explicit row-level `reasoning_effort`, then row `enable_thinking`, then that pipeline's default. Keep the mapping in that pipeline's `sampling.py` (or equivalent) and document it in `docs/<pipeline>/`. Pass the effort string into that resolve step. Do not collapse it to a bool with `thinking_enabled` before the pipeline maps it. A pipeline that only exposes on/off still accepts every OpenAI name and maps onto its supported pair (for Qwen3.6-35B-A3B, `none` and `high`).

Wrong: reject an unsupported effort, infer thinking from the suffix spelling alone, remap the sampling card when effort changes, or treat Gemma's `minimal` as thinking on.

Right: resolve the effort onto a supported name, then open or close thinking from that name. A variant that stores `reasoning_effort` is the omitted-request default for that id.

A prompt of any length up to the pipeline's configured maximum is accepted. That maximum is the served context window. The cache length allocated at open is not a reason to reject the prompt. A prompt past that maximum is rejected. `truncation` `auto` may drop the oldest non-system messages until the prompt fits the maximum. An omitted `truncation` does not drop them.

When the prompt fits the maximum and does not fit the current working set, grow that working set from the ledger and then run prefill. The grow stays in that pipeline. It is not a shared policy on `LanguagePipeline`.

## Memory

`src/favlo/memory.py` is the only allocator. `memory` is the process singleton. Device, residency set, and command queues stay process-owned. Heaps are per owner, kind (`resident`, `scratch`, `private`), and storage (`shared` or `private`). Do not call `newBufferWithLength` or keep a second allocator.

`open_runtime` accepts `owner`. The listener calls `memory.set_max_memory` before that open. Stamp `_owner` before the first allocate:

```python
self._owner = owner or f"{stem}:{id(self)}"
```

`stem` is the flag stem (`qwen36-35b-a3b`). Every `allocate` and `upload` in that opening uses that string. `sweep` matches the whole string. `close` sweeps it in `finally`, including when `open` fails after a span exists. A missing record raises `FileNotFoundError` before `Gpu()`. The listener passes `mint_owner(stem)` when the signature includes `owner`, records `runtime._owner`, and sweeps that opening after `close`.

The serve process keeps the ledger. This process allocates its own Metal buffers. `allocate`, `zeros`, `release`, and `sweep` report the owner and the byte counts on the worker pipe. Do not open that pipe. `budget()` asks the serve process and waits. The answer is reclaimable pages plus this daemon's live, pending, and returning bytes, including every child. `memory.set_max_memory` caps that answer. `room()` is `max(0, budget() - live - pending)`. Read those two. Do not parse `--max-memory`, and do not keep `_memory_cap` or a private `parse_memory`. An in-process `complete` calls `memory.set_max_memory` before the first `budget()`.

The one knob is the context working set. Weights plus that working set must fit in `room()` before the first commit. The prompt keeps its length. Read the budget at open and again before growing the cache, including before a prefill chunk. A larger answer may raise the working set. Do not shrink it while a request is in flight. At open, shrink the working set down to the minimum that still runs the prompt. When that minimum does not fit, raise `RuntimeError` and name the prompt length. Do not drop layers, stand in a host GEMV, or change the packed quant. 16 GB is the product floor. It is not free memory and it is not `budget()`.

Spans go through `device_buffer(owner, nbytes)`. `release` returns a span the caller has dropped. Leave a buffer a later command or a live local still names. `sweep` waits out tracked command buffers, then frees that owner's spans and idle heaps.

`close` sweeps the owner. The release is done when that owner has no live span and no command still holds a freed span. `memory.wait_released` returns on that check. Pending and returning bytes stay in the budget until the kernel reports them. They do not keep the process alive, and they do not keep the next call queued. The daemon does not admit the next call while an allocation or a release is still open. A short reclaim calls `note_returning` for the bytes still held, then drops the child. `confirm_released` and `reclaimable_bytes` stay on raw Mach counts.

Wrong: `self.max_memory = cap or budget()` stored once at open, a second `min` with a private cap, or a prompt rejected because the cache is still at its opening length.

Right: `memory.budget()` on each sizing decision, and a longer cache when that answer grew.

Decode streaming always uses a separate thread and a queue. The decode loop enqueues the token id and returns. It does not decode text, format an event, or write the socket. The stream thread reads that queue, turns ids into text, and writes the events. The enqueue does not wait for that write, so a slow client does not stall the next GPU step. The decode thread joins the stream thread after generation returns. A dead client stops the decode loop on the next enqueue.

Wrong: the decode loop writes the event before the next GPU step.

Right: the decode loop puts the token id on a queue, and a stream thread writes the event.

## Progress (CLI)

Every served language pipeline keeps the pretty/plain CLI moving for the whole request. `open_runtime` stores the listener `console` on the runtime; load, generate, and close talk only through that object. Do not invent a second status channel. A local helper module is fine when it calls the same `favlo.console` API.

Progress uses `favlo.console` only. The pipeline does not `print` its own status rows and does not write tok/s by hand. Phases are `load`, `prefill`, and `decode` on the serve path (`download` and `quantize` on setup). Required notes on every generate, in order: `prompt` (full prompt length), then `prefix_load` when a stored prefix was loaded, then `cache` (hit length). Optional notes are `prefix_store`, `prefix_store_failed`, `mtp_depth`, `mtp`, `incoming`, `output`, and `context`. Use `console.note` (or `format_note` when the console is absent). Pretty folds those into the daemon log. While a listener request is open, prefill and decode summaries wait and print as one durable line when the request closes. A zero-duration phase still closes with `format_rate` (rate 0).

No progress write blocks the hot path. Prefill and decode store `progress.done` and return. Opening a phase, closing a phase, and a diagnostic note are enqueued. The console thread formats the line and writes it. The enqueue returns without waiting for that write. A slow pipe or a slow terminal does not stall the next GPU step. The decode thread does not join the console thread between kernels.

Load: `begin("load", total=bytes, unit="bytes")` while the resident record (and any atlas) is read into device buffers. Advance `done` by each sequential read as bytes land. End with a short loaded summary naming the record and the live context. A silent load with a stuck bar is a bug.

Prefill: after prefix align, when the uncached suffix length is greater than zero, `begin("prefill", total=uncached_tok, unit="tok")`. Do not open a prefill row for a full cache hit. Keep the row on the runtime so the chunk loop can reach it. Advance `done` as work finishes, not once at the end: after each chunk, and inside a multi-layer chunk as each finished layer lands (map finished layers onto token progress; do not wait for the whole suffix). Monotonic only; never move `done` backward. Cap at `total`. The prefill clock starts at the uncached forward and ends when the first output token is sampled. The forward that produced that token's logits stays inside prefill. The prompt prefix write stays outside. Close with `format_rate("prefill", uncached_tok, seconds)` on that first sample, then clear the stored row.

Decode: after that first sample, `begin("decode", total=0, unit="tok")` when `console.live_decode` is true (pretty). An open-ended row uses a spinner. Set `progress.done` to the completion length after each kept token on the decode/GPU path. The decode clock starts at that first sample and ends at the last. The prompt prefix write stays outside it. A send between samples stays inside decode. The last send and the finished-sequence prefix write stay outside. Close with `format_rate("decode", completion_tok, seconds)`. On cancel or disconnect, end every open row with an empty summary.

A generate that opens load/prefill/decode but leaves `done` at 0 until `end`, or that only prints the close line, is unfinished. The live pretty bar and the plain ticks must move while kernels run.

Wrong: `print` a tok/s line from the forward; skip `begin`/`end`; open prefill with `total=0` on a full hit; advance the bar only after the whole prefill or only from the stream thread; leave `console` unset on the runtime; wait on a phase write before the next GPU step; hand-roll a rate string the harness cannot parse.

Right: store the listener `console` at open; emit `prompt` / `prefix_load` / `cache`; open load, prefill (when uncached > 0), and decode through `console.begin`; tick `progress.done` from the load reads, the prefill chunk/layer loop, and each kept decode token; close with `format_rate`. The console thread writes.

NumPy stays off the hot serving path. Prefill, decode, routing, expert grouping, and sampling do not import it, build an array with it, or read a device buffer back through it. The sampled token id may come back as bytes. Checkpoint packing, prefix packing, and the kernel self-checks are not that path.

Wrong: a prefill span that downloads expert ids into a NumPy array and rebuilds the slot tables on the host.

Right: the route result stays on device, and a Metal kernel writes the slot tables the gather and scatter already read.

Quantize from BF16 is CPU-bound and parallel. The pool size is `os.process_cpu_count()`, or `os.cpu_count()` when that count is missing. That count is the worker count. Each worker sets `VECLIB_MAXIMUM_THREADS`, `OMP_NUM_THREADS`, and `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS` to 1, so the pool fills the cores and a matrix product inside one worker does not start another team.

Wrong: packing one matrix at a time, a pool capped below the CPU count, or each worker launching a full BLAS team.

Right: one worker per CPU, one BLAS thread inside that worker, packing the BF16 checkout in parallel.

The packed weight file is read from SSD into one resident Metal buffer. Each read is a sequential piece of at least 8 MiB, written into that buffer. Each matrix stays at its byte offset, and a launch binds the buffer there. A layer file that stays on SSD is read the same way into the slot that runs it. The device holds one copy of the bytes it will run.

Wrong: `read_bytes()` of the weight file, a Python slice per matrix, and `upload_group` into a new heap for each plane.

Right: one buffer, an 8 MiB read into that buffer, and `setBuffer:offset:` at the plane.

MAF and other clients pass those fields through. They do not scan for markup, close a trace, or copy reasoning into the reply. The chat client in `src/favlo/chat_client.py` is that pass-through. A harness does not add its own parser for markup.

Wrong: a client that treats `reasoning_content` as `content`, a chat adapter that drops `reasoning_content` when building Responses input, a shared sampler in `LanguagePipeline`, a chat path copied from another pipeline, or Chat Completions implemented in the pipeline.

Right: the pipeline writes the Response from its own markup; the listener wraps Chat Completions over Responses (including historical reasoning); `ChatWire` fills `content` / `tool_calls` / `reasoning_content`; the client stores those fields as returned.

After a change to this shell, run:

```text
uv run pytest tests/test_language_pipeline.py
uv run python -m harness.language_pipeline
```

The harness is the one agent for the registry default chat model and `bge-m3-int8`. It does not take the machine lock. `harness.server` waits until no live pid holds `artifacts/pipeline-test.lock`, then uses one `favlo serve` on `127.0.0.1:8129`. Exit 0 means `check_embeddings` ran and every paraphrase cosine beat every cross-topic cosine. A kernel or quantize edit that leaves `handle_responses`, the disconnect shell, and the reply alone does not run the harness.

A command that compiles shaders, loads a checkpoint, or uses the GPU in its own process uses `python -m favlo.smoke_lock run`. `python -m harness`, `python -m harness.coding`, `python -m harness.needle`, and `python -m harness.worktree` call `python -m favlo.smoke_lock attach`. That reuses `127.0.0.1:8129` and does not take the lock. When the named pipeline is resident and its sources are newer than that worker, the model is unloaded before the command. A prefix check unloads the resident model after the first turn so the next turn loads the prefix from disk. Do not kill serve to free the GPU. Close during a live hold is 409.

Wrong: a shared sampler in `LanguagePipeline`, or a second harness per language model.

Right: `handle_responses` raises `RequestClosed`, and the shell clears `runtime._stop`.
